---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import DownloadButton from '../../components/DownloadButton.astro';
import { appSchema } from '../../lib';

const rows = [
  ['How it works', 'HTTP proxy: traffic is routed through Proxyman', 'Per-app network control, no proxy'],
  ['Throttling', 'Throttles traffic going through the proxy', 'Throttles only the apps you choose'],
  ['HTTPS', 'Install and trust Proxyman’s certificate to see HTTPS', 'Sees simulator requests with no certificate or proxy settings'],
  ['Failures', 'Breakpoints, map local and scripting', 'Airplane Mode, refused connections, time-outs, dropped connections, outages that come and go, fail or mock one endpoint'],
  ['Offline in the simulator', 'Not a device-like offline state', 'NWPathMonitor and SCNetworkReachability report no network'],
  ['Physical devices', 'Yes, through the proxy', 'Mac apps and iOS Simulator apps'],
];
---
<Base
  title="Proxyman throttling alternative for iOS Simulator and Mac"
  description="Lagsmith vs. Proxyman for network throttling: slow down one app without a proxy or certificates, with Airplane Mode, failure modes and MCP for AI agents."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Compare', href: '/network-link-conditioner-alternative' }, { name: 'Lagsmith vs. Proxyman', href: '/vs/proxyman' }]}
  jsonLd={[appSchema()]}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <h1>Lagsmith vs. Proxyman for throttling</h1>
      <p class="lead">Proxyman is a polished, modern HTTP debugging proxy for Mac. It can throttle, but through its proxy, and it needs a certificate to see HTTPS. Lagsmith focuses on network conditions: one app at a time, no proxy, no certificates.</p>
      <div class="actions"><DownloadButton /></div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="table-compare">
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Proxyman</th><th>Lagsmith</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{rows.map(([k, a, b]) => <tr><td>{k}</td><td>{a}</td><td>{b}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <h2>When Proxyman is the better fit</h2>
      <p>For full HTTP debugging, scripting and inspecting traffic from physical devices, Proxyman is excellent. Lagsmith exports HAR files that open in Proxyman, so the two work well together.</p>
      <h2>When to use Lagsmith</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You want to throttle or break one app’s network while everything else stays fast.</li>
        <li>You want to skip installing certificates in each simulator.</li>
        <li>You need device-like offline behaviour in the simulator.</li>
        <li>You want UI tests or an AI agent to set network conditions.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>See also <a href="/network-link-conditioner-alternative">Lagsmith vs. Network Link Conditioner</a> and <a href="/vs/charles-proxy">Lagsmith vs. Charles Proxy</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Cta />
</Base>
